<Base title="Unifying Store Transfers — Raghav Dharani" description="A public-safe product decision record about modernizing fragmented store transfer workflows." canonical="/case-study/">
  <article class="case-page">
    <div class="case-return"><a href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a><span class="overline">KWI / Product decision record</span></div>
    <header class="case-page-hero"><p class="overline accent">In development · Enterprise merchandising</p><h1>One transfer workflow.<br /><em>Many operating realities.</em></h1><p>Four legacy tools covered different parts of inventory movement. The design challenge was not simply consolidation. It was finding the shared logic without erasing the exceptions that real stores need.</p></header>
    <div class="case-facts"><div><span class="overline">Role</span><p>Product strategy, discovery, workflow design and phased rollout planning</p></div><div><span class="overline">Users</span><p>Store teams creating, shipping, receiving and reconciling transfers</p></div><div><span class="overline">Status</span><p>In development. No launch or migration outcomes claimed.</p></div></div>
    <div class="case-sections">
      <section><span class="index">01 / Context</span><div><h2>Fragmentation was obvious. Variance was harder.</h2><p>Creation, bulk updates, receiving and box-level receiving lived in separate tools. But teams work in different ways: some scan inventory, some use picklists, and others need box-level processes or partial receipts.</p><p>Unifying the interface without addressing these differences would simply carry the fragmentation forward.</p></div></section>
      <section><span class="index">02 / Decision</span><div><h2>Standardize the state. Preserve useful flexibility.</h2><p>The proposed product shares transfer states, permission boundaries and core validations. It accommodates different receiving methods, discrepancies, quantity variances and controlled post-booking changes.</p><div class="decision-pairs"><div><span class="overline">Consistent</span><p>States, validation, permissions, discrepancy visibility</p></div><div><span class="overline">Configurable</span><p>Receiving methods, thresholds, entry paths and exception handling</p></div></div></div></section>
      <section><span class="index">03 / Method</span><div><h2>Prototype where things go wrong.</h2><p>I mapped the end-to-end journey, documented rules and API expectations, then used prototypes to challenge assumptions with Product, Engineering and QA. AI coding tools helped accelerate exploration of workflows and edge cases; product judgments stayed grounded in discovery and review.</p></div></section>
      <section><span class="index">04 / Outcome model</span><div><h2>The release isn’t the result. Adoption is.</h2><p>The product is still in development, so I do not claim reduced errors or completed migration. Measures worth watching include transfer completion time, receiving accuracy, exceptions, support volume and adoption by workflow.</p></div></section>
    </div>
    <div class="case-end"><p>The enduring lesson: modernization is as much a change-management challenge as a design exercise.</p><a href="/#work">Return to all work ↗</a></div>
  </article>
</Base>
